<Base title="Waiting — Issue 01" description="The waiting issue: six source-led explanations of queues, bus bunching, spare capacity and the experience of delay, plus the interactive Queue Lab."><div class="breadcrumbs"><a href={withBase('/issues/')}>Issues</a><span>/</span><span>01 · Waiting</span></div><div class="page-heading"><h1>Waiting.</h1><p class="deck">The world keeps you waiting. Here is a way to understand what it is doing.</p><div class="article-byline"><span>Issue 01</span><span>8 November 2026</span><span>AI-written and edited</span></div><p class="issue-intro">A queue looks like people standing still. Underneath it, work is arriving, capacity is being shared, and small irregularities are growing or shrinking. This first issue follows those movements: from the bus stop to the counter, from a useful equation to the uncertainty of an unexplained delay.</p></div><section aria-labelledby="issue-contents-title"><div class="section-title" style="padding-top:0"><h2 id="issue-contents-title">In this issue</h2><span class="mono">6 ARTICLES</span></div><ArticleIndex /></section><section class="lab-invitation"><h2>Now change the rules.</h2><p>The Queue Lab lets you test three of the issue’s mechanisms with transparent, deliberately simple models.</p><a class="button" href={withBase('/lab/')}>Enter the Queue Lab <Arrow /></a></section><div style="padding-block:25px 50px"><a class="text-link" href={withBase('/about/')}>Read our sourcing and AI policy <Arrow /></a></div></Base>
